The  device driver supports the 3Com EtherLink III family of Eth‐
ernet cards.  The 3c515 is an ISA 10/100 card with  DMA  capabil‐
ity.   The  chipset  is  similar  to that of the 3c905, with some
changes to make it work with the more  limited  ISA  bus  address
space.   This  card  is supported, although DMA is not used.  The
EISA and PCI 3c59x devices use an older DMA engine which  is  not
capable  of multi‐segment DMA.  DMA on these devices is not used.
The 3c529 is a MCA device, and  doesn’t  support  DMA.   The  PCI
3c90x  devices  have  multi‐segment  DMA capability, which is not
supported by the driver.  To use the DMA  capabilities  of  these
cards,  the  driver must be used.  The PCI 3c90xB devices are not
supported by the driver, as they do not include support for  pro‐
grammed  I/O.   These  devices  are supported by the driver.  The
3c575 is a CardBus device, and is supported by driver.  There are
3 main chipset classes supported by the driver.  Each  has  their
own  media selection capabilities.  The first class is the class.
This includes the 3c509, 3c509B, 3c529, 3c579, 3c562, and  3c589.
These devices can support 10BASE‐T, 10BASE2, and 10BASE5.  Avail‐
able media will be displayed when the device is found by the ker‐
nel.   The  second  class is the class.  This includes the 3c592,
3c579, 3c590, and 3c595.  This class also includes the  3c900‐TPO
and 3c900‐COMBO; they use the chipset, but use Vortex‐style media
selection.  These devices have many different media types varying
by  model.   Some  models have an external MII connector for con‐
necting an external PHY.  This is supported by means of the media
type.  Available media will be displayed when the device is found
by the kernel.  The third class is the class.  This includes  the
3c515,  3c905,  and 3c574.  These devices support media selection
via MII.  The 3c515 and 3c905 have an and the  3c574  has  a  for
this purpose.  See and for more information.  Supported cards in‐
